yghur_Style.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Mizu basyo at Japanese Wikipedia","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dapanji_Uyghur_Style.jpg"}},"ingredient/alkali":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/alkali","n":"Alkali","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["In chemistry, an alkali ( ) or base"]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-alkali"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alkali"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine",[["Huangqiao sesame cake","dish/huangqiao-sesame-cake"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Caramel","ingredient/caramel"],["Lard","ingredient/lard"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"],["Sesame","ingredient/sesame"],["Suet","ingredient/suet"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Yeast","ingredient/yeast"]]}]},"dish/ikan-bakar":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/ikan-bakar","n":"Ikan bakar","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Malay cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Ikan bakar is a Malay dish, prepared with charcoal-grilled fish or other forms of seafood. Ikan bakar literally means \"grilled fish\" in the Malay and Indonesian languages. Ikan bakar differs from other grilled fish dishes in that it often contains flavorings like bumbu, kecap manis, and sambal, and is covered in a banana leaf and cooked on a charcoal fire."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-ikan-bakar"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ikan_bakar"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Malay cuisine","cuisine/malay-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":7,"links":[["Banana leaf","ingredient/banana-leaf"],["Charcoal","ingredient/charcoal"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Fish","ingredient/fish"],["Grilled","ingredient/grilled"],["Lemongrass","ingredient/lemongrass"],["Shallots","ingredient/shallots"]]},{"h":"More from Malay cuisine","links":[["Akok (food)","dish/akok-food"],["Karas (food)","dish/karas-food"],["Ketupat","dish/ketupat"],["Lekor","dish/lekor"],["Mee siput","dish/mee-siput"],["Nasi Ayam","dish/nasi-ayam"],["Nasi kerabu","dish/nasi-kerabu"],["Nasi tumpang","dish/nasi-tumpang"],["Roti jala","dish/roti-jala"],["Sata (food)","dish/sata-food"],["Tahu goreng","dish/tahu-goreng"],["Ulam (salad)","dish/ulam-salad"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/e/ec/Gurame_bakar_kecap_2.JPG/500px-Gurame_bakar_kecap_2.JPG","w":500,"h":364,"by":"Gunawan Kartapranata","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gurame_bakar_kecap_2.JPG"}},"ingredient/juice":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/juice","n":"Juice","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Juice is a drink made from the extraction or pressing of the natural liquid contained in fruit and vegetables. It can also refer to liquids that are flavored with concentrate or other biological food sources like meat or seafood, such as clam juice. Juice is commonly consumed as a beverage or used as an ingredient or flavoring in foods or other beverages, such as smoothies. Juice emerged as a popular beverage choice after the development of pasteurization methods enabled its preservation without using fermentation (which is used in wine production). The largest fruit juice consumers are New Zealand (nearly a cup, or 8 ounces, each day) and Colombia (more than three quarters of a cup each day). Fruit juice consumption on average increases with a country's income level."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-juice"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juice"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine",[["Mors (drink)","dish/mors-drink"]]],["Ukrainian cuisine","cuisine/ukrainian-cuisine",[["Mors (drink)","dish/mors-drink"]]]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/f/fd/Orange_juice_1.jpg/500px-Orange_juice_1.jpg","w":500,"h":711,"by":"Agricultural Research Service","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Orange_juice_1.jpg"}},"dish/rujak-soto":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/rujak-soto","n":"Rujak soto","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Indonesian cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Rujak soto is an Indonesian traditional salad made up of unique blend between beef soto and rujak cingur. A local speciality in which the vegetables (water spinach and bean sprouts) rujak served with lontong rice cake in petis sauce poured with soto soup. It is a delicacy of Javanese (Osing) from Banyuwangi, East Java. Generally, rujak soto is served along with es temulawak—that made up of Curcuma zanthorrhiza."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-rujak-soto"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rujak_soto"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":13,"links":[["Banana","ingredient/banana"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Celery","ingredient/celery"],["Galangal","ingredient/galangal"],["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Lemongrass","ingredient/lemongrass"],["Lontong","ingredient/lontong"],["Palm sugar","ingredient/palm-sugar"],["Scallion","ingredient/scallion"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Tofu","ingredient/tofu"],["Tripe","ingredient/tripe"],["Turmeric","ingredient/turmeric"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Daily Cooking Quest","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"dailycookingquest.com","note":"Indonesian home cook Anita's blog with a large archive of Indonesian recipes.","url":"https://dailycookingquest.com/cuisines/indonesian.html","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"Mo